# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/

# Secrets.
#
# THE BARE `.env` THAT USED TO BE THE WHOLE RULE MATCHED ONLY THAT ONE NAME. It did not match
# `backend/.env.supabase.bak`, a real file in this tree that held the DATABASE_URL and password of
# the project that hosted this deployment until 2026-08-22, plus JWT_SECRET, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, three Gemini
# keys and ADMIN_PASSWORD — the last of which is reused AS the database password. This repository
# has no `.git` yet, so the first `git init && git add .` would have committed every one of them,
# and SECURITY.md §2.2 is explicit that the database credentials bypass the entire role ladder in
# one step.
#
# So the rule is now "every .env variant, whatever it is called", with the example templates
# explicitly re-admitted because they are meant to be read and are verified blank.
#
# A .gitignore line is NOT the remedy for a key that already exists on disk in plaintext — it only
# stops the next mistake. Anything in a file matched here has to be rotated at the provider.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# The database provider's CLI writes its machine-local state here (project link, version cache).
supabase/.temp/

# The AWS console names every downloaded access-key CSV like this (`<user>_accessKeys.csv`), and a
# download dropped into the repo root is one `git add .` away from publishing an admin credential.
# The pattern, not one filename: the next key download must not depend on somebody re-learning this.
*accessKeys*.csv

