TRANSCRIPTION_SERVICE.md

# Downloaded boundary sources for scripts/build_boundaries.py — 63 MB of published GeoJSON that the
# script re-fetches with --fetch. The GENERATED assets under frontend/public/boundaries and
# android/.../res/raw ARE committed; these inputs are not.
.boundary-cache/

# Transient git worktrees created for isolated parallel agents. Build state, never source.
.claude/worktrees/

# ── DOWNLOADED NATIVE ARTIFACTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `android/app/libs/sherpa-onnx-static-link-onnxruntime-1.13.5.aar` is 37 MB of downloaded release
# asset, and on 2026-08-15 it was untracked, uncovered by this file, and one `git add -A` away from
# entering the history permanently — where it could not be removed without rewriting every commit
# after it.
#
# IT IS NOT MEANT TO BE COMMITTED, AND THAT IS A DESIGN DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
# `docs/PLAN-AI-TIERS-AND-CUSTOM-SECTIONS.md` §5 records why: bundling the speech runtime takes the
# ARM-pair APK from 26.2 MB to 79.6 MB — 3.03×, measured off eight packaged APKs — so the runtime is
# an offer the designer accepts on a connection they choose, not weight every designer in the fleet
# downloads on prepaid mobile data to serve the few who go to the field. An AAR in the repository is
# the first step back toward bundling it by accident.
#
# The upstream artifact is not on `google()` or `mavenCentral()` under any spelling (twelve tried,
# every one a 404), so it cannot be a build-time dependency either; it is fetched at runtime and its
# SHA-256 is pinned in the APK and verified on disk before anything is loaded. See
# `docs/ASR-RUNTIME-DOWNLOAD-CONTRACT.md`.
#
# Scoped to the directory rather than to `*.aar` everywhere, so a future AAR that genuinely SHOULD be
# vendored has to be an explicit decision rather than something this rule silently swallows.
android/app/libs/

# Signing keys. These must never be committed — see android/app/build.gradle.kts.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
